WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:08.141
Marques:<i> Before cell phones,
 it was a very different world.</i>

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:11.377
Couldn't really walk around.
You were kinda stuck
to wherever the phone was.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.781
Absolutely no privacy,
and anyone could pick up
the phone

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.449
<i> and listen
 to your conversation.</i>

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.453
Woman:
<i> Growing up, I always really
 wanted a cell phone,</i>

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.221
but my parents wouldn't
let me have one.

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:25.658
Getting my first cell phone
was freaking mind-blowing.

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:28.327
One of my earlier cell phones
was, like, a flip phone.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.197
There is something
really satisfying
about cracking it open.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
- ( snapping )
- The cell phone gives you
  this sense of independence.

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.437
I thought I was the coolest.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.839
I thought I was
a super tech girl.

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:43.043
Oh, my God. My first cell phone
was the ultimate freedom.

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:51.751
Marques:
<i> I'm Marques Brownlee,
 and I review dope new tech.</i>

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:53.519
<i> But on this show,
 I'm rewinding the clock</i>

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:55.655
<i> to discover the tech
 of the past</i>

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:59.092
<i> that changed
 our lives forever.</i>

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:02.795
<i> This is
 "Retro Tech: DynaTAC."</i>

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.597
Hey, what's up, guys?
MKBHD here.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.499
As you already know,
I'm pretty into tech,

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:09.168
and specifically
a lot of smartphones.

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:10.503
My first ever cell phone

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:13.106
was this little blue
Samsung flip phone,

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.675
probably back
in, like, 2005.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:21.347
And even that
was 20 years after
the first ever cell phone

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.817
the DynaTAC 8000x
was released.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.153
So this is not
the original box it came in,

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:31.224
but inside this box
is the OG DynaTAC.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:32.625
Let's get into this.

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:36.596
That's a lot of leather,

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:39.132
and you actually get
a little leather smell.

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:42.201
I hope that means
it's real leather.

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.437
This is the DynaTAC in here.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.339
Good old Velcro. Whoa.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:49.475
That is crazy.

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:53.713
And this is your
antenna-looking thing.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.516
It has a digital screen.
The buttons are pretty soft.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.251
It really does have
some heft to it.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.821
It'd be kinda funny to see
how many phones today

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:05.792
would equal the thickness
of the DynaTAC.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:13.199
One, two, three.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:17.537
Five, seven, ten
of today's smart phones thick.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.771
But that's how far we've come.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:19.839
If that isn't Moore's Law
for you,

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:20.874
I don't know what is.

00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:24.677
This battery comes off.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.479
"May explode
if disposed in fire."

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.348
Slide that back on.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:34.320
So these phones operated
on the very first

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.489
wireless network
ever created,

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:39.225
which we'll now
refer back to as 1G.

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:42.261
1G networks are not
still operational.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:44.530
They haven't been in,
like, 20 years,

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:46.866
but I'm kinda thinking
of things

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:50.803
that I think people would say
on 1980s phone calls.

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:54.440
Hey, you gonna be
at jazzercise today, Bill?

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:56.676
My fax should have
gone through by now.
Hopefully you got that.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.378
- Hey, are you home yet?
- Yeah, I just got here.

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:03.349
Can you set up the VCR
to record "Golden Girls"
on the VHS today?

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:06.319 align:start size:97% position:3%
- For sure.
- I'm probably gonna miss it.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.387
Bill, relax.
I sent the fax.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.889
You'll get it soon, okay?

00:03:11.957 --> 00:03:13.526
<i> It's hard to imagine
 a time now</i>

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.528
<i> without a cell phone
 in every pocket.</i>

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:17.663
<i> So I need to know,
 how did the DynaTAC create</i>

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:20.633
<i> an entirely new world
 of portable communication</i>

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:22.668
<i> where it didn't exist before?</i>

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:24.770 line:0%
First of all, welcome.
Thanks for joining me.

00:03:24.803 --> 00:03:27.273 align:start position:0% line:0%
- Thank you, my friend.
- I want to start by asking you

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:29.242 align:start position:0%
to check underneath
your chair.

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.978
I can do that, I guess.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:35.281 line:0%
Oh! That's cra--
yo, I'm done. I'm done.

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:37.883 line:0%
Look how big this antenna is.
This is huge.

00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:39.452
Man, this phone is mad heavy.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:41.254
Like, if you ever
got into a problem,

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:42.888
you could throw it at somebody
and actually win a fight.

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:46.525 line:0%
Like, this thing is just baller.
It's so cool!

00:03:46.558 --> 00:03:47.760
Marques:
So I want to take it back

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.629
before the days
of any cellular phones.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.297
What was that world like?

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.300
( phones ringing )

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:55.735
Lisa:
<i> Before there were cell phones,</i>

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.770
<i> you could make a phone call
 from home</i>

00:03:57.803 --> 00:03:59.672
before you left your house.

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:01.974
<i> But if there was an emergency
 or if your car broke down,</i>

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:04.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
-<i> you had to use a pay phone. </i>
- This is an emergency.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.045
Give me the local
fire department paramedics.

00:04:06.078 --> 00:04:08.848
Until the car phone came out.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:12.818
- ( phone ringing )
- Brad:<i> Motorola was
  this tech company,</i>

00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:16.055
and what Motorola was developing
were these car phones.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.591
The car phone
was the first mobile phone,

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:21.927
and they were only mobile
in the sense that you could
fit them into your trunk.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.630 line:0%
Announcer:
<i> More and more people have
 a phone in their car,</i>

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:27.767 line:0%
<i> like this unique cellular
 portable made by Motorola.</i>

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.935
Michael:
<i> They were massive, massive
 pieces of equipment</i>

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:31.570
<i> that drew a lot of power.</i>

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:33.339
Announcer:
<i> And they are a hot item.</i>

00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:35.474
<i> A year from now,
 every metropolitan area</i>

00:04:35.507 --> 00:04:37.877
<i> of the nation will have
 cellular phone service.</i>

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.546
Brad:
<i> So, the challenge
 with the tech</i>

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:43.482
<i> was how to fit
 all of the crap</i>

00:04:43.515 --> 00:04:45.651
<i> in the trunk of the car,</i>

00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:47.987
<i> the antenna
 on the top of the car,</i>

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.624
into an actual portable
handheld cell phone.

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:54.727
There was a team within Motorola
led by Dr. Martin Cooper.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.564
<i> Dr. Cooper emerged
 as the leading proponent of,</i>

00:04:58.597 --> 00:04:59.732
<i> "Hey, people are gonna
 want to buy</i>

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:01.934
<i> portable cellular telephones.</i>

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:04.370
We need to build them.

00:05:04.403 --> 00:05:06.739
<i> And we need to build them
 in a massively short
 amount of time."</i>

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.475
<i> Because they knew that
there was gonna be competition.</i>

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:10.943
And I think
one of the engineers said,

00:05:10.976 --> 00:05:13.045
"Oh, that sounds great.
I'll get around to that."

00:05:13.078 --> 00:05:15.014
And Dr. Cooper said,
"You don't understand.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:16.916
We have to build this
in six weeks."

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.751
What's crazy
is they were able to do it.

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:20.320
Whoa.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:23.923
<i> Marty Cooper and his team
 at Motorola</i>

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.892
<i> were in a race
 against rival phone company
 Bell Laboratories</i>

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:30.062
<i> to come up with the world's
 first cell phone.</i>

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:32.798
<i> So, who better to tell
 that story</i>

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.767
<i> than the legend himself?</i>

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.937
Martin Cooper:
At the beginning,
it was a great curiosity.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.139 line:0%
I don't think anybody
ever believed

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:43.409 line:0%
that everybody
would have them--

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:44.710 line:0%
except we did.

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:47.880 align:start position:0%
What was the first
phone call that you made

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:49.648
after the successful creation

00:05:49.681 --> 00:05:51.384
of that first DynaTAC
prototype?

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.885
I must tell you
that I hadn't planned

00:05:52.918 --> 00:05:54.687
on who I was gonna call.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.623
<i> But walking down
 6th Avenue in New York,</i>

00:05:57.656 --> 00:05:58.991
<i> and it occurred to me,</i>

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:01.026
<i> "Why don't I call
 my counterpart?"</i>

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:04.063
<i> His name was Dr. Joel Angel
 at Bell Laboratories.</i>

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:07.600
<i> And I said, "Hi, Joel.
 This is Marty Cooper."</i>

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.001
<i> And he says, "Hi, Marty."</i>

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:12.037
<i> "Joel, I'm calling you
 from a cell phone.</i>

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.739
<i> But a real cell phone.</i>

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:19.478
<i> A handheld personal
 portable cell phone.</i>

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:20.980
<i> What do you think of that?"</i>

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:23.616
<i> Silence on the other
 end of the line.</i>

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:26.118
<i> I could only imagine
 what he was thinking,</i>

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:28.721
<i> and I suspect he was
 gritting his teeth.</i>

00:06:30.155 --> 00:06:32.158
<i> And that's when things
 took off.</i>

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.626
Did you ever imagine
the technology

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.628
advancing this far?

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.862 align:start size:56% position:44%
Not a chance.

00:06:36.895 --> 00:06:39.799
Today, there are more
portable phones

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:42.635
in the world
than there are people.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:44.603
So we never could've
imagined that.

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:47.840
Amazing. I can remember
the first phone I ever got,

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:49.642
the first smartphone
I ever got,

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.610
the first time I ever
pointed a camera at myself

00:06:51.643 --> 00:06:53.913
and talked about how much
I loved my new phone.

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.782 align:start position:0%
All that stuff
comes from the DynaTAC,

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.617
so I feel like I owe you
a thank you, too.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.686 size:81% position:41%
You're very, very welcome.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:02.621
Marques:
<i> The DynaTAC prototype</i>

00:07:02.654 --> 00:07:06.792
<i> was nothing like anyone
 had ever seen or sold before.</i>

00:07:06.825 --> 00:07:09.628 line:0%
<i> The end result
 was 1.75 pounds,</i>

00:07:09.661 --> 00:07:11.864 line:0%
<i> stood 13 inches high,</i>

00:07:11.897 --> 00:07:13.933 line:0%
<i> stored 30 numbers,</i>

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:15.501 line:0%
<i> took 10 hours to charge</i>

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.870 line:0%
<i> for about 30 minutes
 of talk time,</i>

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:24.043 line:0%
<i> and cost a hefty $3,995.</i>

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:25.511 line:0%
<i> Adjusted for inflation,</i>

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:28.647 line:0%
<i> that's over $10,000 today.</i>

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.883
<i> By today's standards,
this might not seem impressive,</i>

00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:32.885
<i> but in the early '80s,
 the DynaTAC</i>

00:07:32.918 --> 00:07:35.488
<i> was a groundbreaking
 piece of technology,</i>

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.857
<i> and the very first
 of its kind.</i>

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.693
<i> And in March of 1984,
 the DynaTAC 8000X</i>

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.127
<i> officially hit the market.</i>

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.762 line:0%
And this is it.

00:07:43.795 --> 00:07:47.766 line:0%
This is a portable
cellular phone.

00:07:47.799 --> 00:07:49.802
When people got hold

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:51.971
<i> of the first portable
 cellular telephone,</i>

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:54.206
<i> they didn't want
 to put them down.</i>

00:07:54.239 --> 00:07:56.642
They became addicted
to that convenience.

00:07:56.675 --> 00:07:59.111
I think it's the greatest thing
that I've ever had,

00:07:59.144 --> 00:08:00.980
and it's something
that I wanted somebody

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:02.948
to come up with
for a long time.

00:08:02.981 --> 00:08:04.717
Brad:
<i> So, after the DynaTAC
 comes out,</i>

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:08.754
cell phones do blow up,
but it's a limited blow-up

00:08:08.787 --> 00:08:11.790
<i> because these phones
 were so expensive.</i>

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:12.992
The prevailing attitude
at the time was

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.093
how are you gonna get people

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:16.929
to buy a $4,000 handset?

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:19.098
<i> This is gonna be a toy
 for the rich</i>

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:21.133
<i> and nobody's gonna
 buy the thing.</i>

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:23.269 line:0%
( tires squeal )

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:27.273 line:0%
Brad:
<i> But once pop culture started
 pushing this stuff out,</i>

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:31.277 line:0%
people were buying
these phones to show out.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.847 line:0%
Very famously, of course,
the film "Wall Street"
featured the phone,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.715
and I remember thinking
as a kid,

00:08:36.748 --> 00:08:38.083
<i> "Wow, I really
 want one of those.</i>

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:39.785
<i> It looks like
 it makes you cool."</i>

00:08:39.818 --> 00:08:43.122 line:0%
I don't care
where or how you get it,

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:44.757 line:0%
just get it.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.091
And there was
"Saved by the Bell."

00:08:46.124 --> 00:08:47.192
The Zack Morris phone.

00:08:47.225 --> 00:08:48.827
I'm, I guess, a little young.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.930
I don't think I've ever seen
"Saved By The Bell."

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:52.765 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Who is Zack Morris?
- What?

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.634
And what is
the Zack Morris phone?

00:08:55.634 --> 00:08:57.169
All right.

00:08:57.202 --> 00:08:59.838
<i> "Saved By The Bell"
 was an iconic</i>

00:08:59.871 --> 00:09:03.175
<i> mid-'90s television show
 where Zack Morris</i>

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:05.711
essentially terrorizes
everyone in the school.

00:09:05.744 --> 00:09:07.346 line:0%
<i> He would have this phone,</i>

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:10.115 line:0%
<i> making calls
 and doing deals,</i>

00:09:10.148 --> 00:09:11.850 line:0%
<i> and essentially
 putting his friends</i>

00:09:11.883 --> 00:09:14.920 line:0%
<i> in very precarious
 predicaments all the time.</i>

00:09:14.953 --> 00:09:16.322 line:0%
I'd like to order
a large pizza

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:18.891 line:0%
and the hottest peppers
you can find.

00:09:18.924 --> 00:09:21.994
When kids saw Zack Morris
using a cell phone,

00:09:22.027 --> 00:09:23.829
<i> we talked about that phone.</i>

00:09:23.862 --> 00:09:25.731
Like, "Yo, we want that."

00:09:25.764 --> 00:09:27.700
Lisa:
<i> That was when we really
 started to see</i>

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:30.169
the DynaTAC 8000X
entering the mainstream

00:09:30.202 --> 00:09:32.171
for the everyday person.

00:09:32.204 --> 00:09:34.673
Marques:
<i> Four decades after
 the DynaTAC 8000X</i>

00:09:34.706 --> 00:09:36.842
<i> was first released,
 mobile phones have become</i>

00:09:36.875 --> 00:09:40.245
<i> the most used electronic
 device in the world.</i>

00:09:40.278 --> 00:09:42.348
<i> But these last 40 years
 haven't been without</i>

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:45.818
<i> some questionable designs
 along the way.</i>

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:48.287 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, we have with us
some of the most interesting

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.623 align:start size:56% position:44%
and unique designs
from that time,

00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:52.858 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
and we also have
fellow YouTuber and friend

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:55.060 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
Austin Evans to help
take a look at those.

00:09:55.093 --> 00:09:58.364 line:0%
<i> Austin has reviewed tech
 for over a decade on YouTube,</i>

00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:00.132
<i> and he especially loves
 all things cellular.</i>

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:01.133
...to this.

00:10:01.166 --> 00:10:04.136 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
This is "Dope Or Nope."

00:10:04.169 --> 00:10:06.138 align:start size:75% position:25%
All right, let's take
a look at the first one.

00:10:06.171 --> 00:10:09.341
This is called the Motorola
StarTAC Rainbow.

00:10:09.374 --> 00:10:12.678 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Is this from
  the '90s, perhaps?
- This is from 1998.

00:10:12.711 --> 00:10:14.680 size:88% position:44%
Go ahead and flip this open.

